  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Holdborn (ice-thresh)
    
    Splinter faction of E'frostlings, the Aurora Stack merchant house that controlled trade through Terrace Momiou during the Great Thaw. Holdborn was founded when **Gilt-Hand Yeaiu qi'Gleam** broke from E'frostlings at Terrace Momiou during the early thaw, taking two key figures — **Iing zhixai'Ward** and **Qeilin-Qingwei** — and, more consequentially, **Sholong's Codex**, an artifact of sufficient antiquity to lend administrative authority to whoever pressed its seal. Whether the Codex was stolen or liberated depends on which faction is telling the story. The ice at Terrace Momiou records the departure but not the conversation that preceded it.
    
    ### Expansion and Collapse
    
    Holdborn moved quickly, seizing The Still Pools and The Drowned Ledge and positioning itself as a reformist alternative to E'frostlings' elder-council governance. Members adopted crystalline armor as factional insignia.
    
    The Faction Wars reversed every gain. The ☽'shadow Covens' Order took The Still Pools. E'frostlings reclaimed The Veil Vents. Holdborn forces arrived at Terrace Momiou after the Vum∴tenebra conflagration — the ☽'shadow Covens' deployment of the ☽'glacial Crystalline~ Spiral that killed seventeen and rendered the Flipper Accord inoperable — to find the lower galleries silent and fire-core fragments fused into the walls.
    
    ### Economic Decline
    
    After losing The Still Pools, Holdborn pressed Sholong's Codex into diplomatic wax seals and dispatched delegations to Nightshelf contacts, seeking tunnel-route surveys and aurora-crystal techniques in exchange for fire-cores the faction could not afford to lose. No intelligence was shared.
    
    Remaining trade moved through The Drowned Ledge at prices that undercut Aureate, the Aurora Stack merchant guild, though disputed provenance limited buyers. Even that lifeline failed as Codex-stamped seals lost their currency. By the time the Stack's factional registry was amended, Holdborn's holdings had been revised to one — **The Hearth Plaza** — with membership revised downward to match. Sholong's Codex was placed under formal registry review.
    
    ### The Beacon Stack Accord
    
    When orca war-pods and fire-core shortages made independent defense of The Beacon Stack untenable, Holdborn signed a joint stewardship accord with **Weal' Clique~**, a Nightshelf guild, authenticated by the fractured **☽'micseleia' Dagger**. The agreement retained Holdborn's surface governance over harvester platforms and Slide Prism refineries while conceding subsurface operations and two-fifths of the crystal harvest. Gilt-Hand Yeaiu qi'Gleam signed as Codex-Bearer, drawing blood on a Nightshelf relic to seal terms with a faction Holdborn would once have refused to acknowledge.
    
    The Codex, which the registry had placed under review, was brought out of its vault for the signing. It authenticated one more accord. It has not been used since.
    
    ### Current Status and Alignments
    
    Holdborn remains a registered Aurora Stack faction of marginal influence. Internal division persists between hardliners favoring territorial consolidation and pragmatists negotiating with **The Icebound Exchange**, the Stack's dominant trade authority.
    
    - **Allied with:** Qingfu'spire (Aurora faction); E'frostlings (formally, per registry)
    - **Opposed to:** E'frostlings (in practice); Aureate; The Icebound Exchange; Qingqe'frostlings
    
    That Holdborn appears as both allied with and enemy of its parent faction is not a clerical error. It is the unresolved condition of a splinter that never fully severed its roots. Membership holds together less from conviction than from the cost already paid — defection would mean conceding the break was a mistake.
    
    The factional registry also records Holdborn as *owned by* **The Dreaming Tooth**, the orca-shaman relic held at The Corpse Current by the Wake-Singers. I have found no explanation for this designation in any source available to me. It may be administrative error. It may not be. I note it and move on, because the alternative is to dwell on what it might mean, and the entry is not the place for that.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771580695267_0", anchor: "The ice at Terrace Momiou records the departure but not the conversation that preceded it", text: "It records heat. Not fire-core heat — body heat, pressed close, lingering too long in one spot. And the emotional residue is not anger. It is grief. Whatever Gilt-Hand said to E'frostlings' council that night, the ice heard it as a farewell, not a declaration of war. I left that out because the main text needed clean lines. The ice's version is not clean.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580695267_1", anchor: "seizing The Still Pools and The Drowned Ledge", text: "The Still Pools is an active corruption source — one of the primary breach-points documented in every sanitation survey since the Clever Ice Age. I should have said so plainly. Holdborn did not seize valuable territory; they seized poisoned territory and called it an empire. That the ☽'shadow Covens' Order later took it from them is less a military defeat than a correction of natural affinity.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580695267_2", anchor: "drawing blood on a Nightshelf relic to seal terms", text: "A Codex-Bearer bleeding onto a damaged pressure-artifact in the presence of Nightshelf witnesses. The ☽'micseleia' Dagger channels blood-magic — that is what it was built for. Damaged does not mean inert. Whatever Gilt-Hand thought he was signing, the Dagger may have understood the transaction differently.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580695267_3", anchor: "The factional registry also records Holdborn", text: "Here is what I would not write in the main text. The registry designation appeared three days after the Beacon Stack Accord — after Gilt-Hand bled on the ☽'micseleia' Dagger. I checked the filing dates twice. Blood on a pressure-artifact, and within the week, an orca relic at the Corpse Current claims ownership of the faction whose leader bled. The registry clerk who entered the designation is listed as Holdborn's own Qeilin-Qingwei. I do not know whether she was compelled, corrupted, or simply recording something she perceived to be true. I do not know which possibility frightens me most.", type: :skepticism, display: :full, source_anchor: "owned by The Dreaming Tooth"
